aa1df1fd8b501042458d49c498446b2e092c7186245390944404d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37831" :question/id #uuid "544f8b26-26dd-59f6-ae3d-e40288df33a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37831" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+1) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+2A-4I-5{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37831/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37831"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-38704/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "03ffd024-3fb4-5b30-a0be-7824b03dde4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38704/selection/bc1aaa9670e8118cdb6ca0d3888d99928c842323e3d10885fe212d998358f89d" :answer/id #uuid "10b1581d-57da-51e2-8979-53c8b6e6d009" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/v