\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+6I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+6I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+6{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 6{A}^{-1} & =8I-A \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{4}{3}I-\\frac{1}{6}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{4}{3},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{6}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{4}{3}+(-\\frac{1}{6})=\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-38704/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-38704"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37779"] :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+9I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+9I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+9{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{8}{9}I-\\frac{1}{9}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{8}{9},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{9},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{8}{9}+(-\\frac{1}{9})=\\frac{7}{9}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-40173/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "6541be73-2bf8-5064-b2ee-f2c7ff192d2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math 