estion/math-academy-id "q-37777" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+4x+3,$ then ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{4}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+4x+3,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $4x$ | $+$ | $13$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+4x+3\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{4}\\,\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | $+$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $+$ | $3{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $3{x}^{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | $4{x}^{3}$ | $-$ | $16{x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $12x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $13{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $12x$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $13{x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $52x$ | $+$ | $39$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  | $-$ | $40x$ | $-$ | $39$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{4}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4} & =q(x)p(x)+(-40x-39),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{2}-4x+13.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{4}=q(A)p(A)+(-40A-39I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & =q(A)p(A)+(-40A-39I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-40A-39I) \\\\  & =-40A-39I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37777/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37777"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-85993/selection/63ab63239396a08a1fef31b9b3d59579f5b179faf95f07ce4081bbb8eb97e7c9" :answer/id #uuid "f8cfb567-7a5f-5795-a799-194d6557d732" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}27 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85993/selection/ede519f9548509695f71af0b4da4133ecee6a7096f8cef693396c93f0c93b6a8" :answer/id #uuid "a0cd1d8a-a720-569a-ab63-08d1c6ecc922" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 27 \\\\ 0 & -72\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85993/selection/0444fcd60c99f1c7d68f1ec72c0edfc4dba58137fbf65ca1a3b6f87a282e3baa" :answer/id #uuid "675a9482-82be-54c9-b3dd-5b826e29f225" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 0 \\\\ 72 & -27\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85993/selection/93b0e7d6c722cdad57903f6e553c2a90130576e683447672e639acdf5731f41b" :answer/id #uuid "25ff0b48-1b15-5ed7-a36a-d036de14995d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85993/selection/ddcdb16f55dd87ecf4915bdee8381889bdbe47ba511833137a4f0eb2ddc785a3" :answer/id #uuid "5484591e-6e12-55fb-82b7-ca236204781f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85993/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3638314-7918-5275-beb0-23fe0c31e1b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-85993/selection/93b0e7d6c722cdad57903f6e553c2a90130576e683447672e639acdf5731f41b" :answer-field/choices ["q-85993/selection/63ab63239396a08a1fef31b9b3d59579f5b179faf95f07ce4081bbb8eb97e7c9" "q-85993/selection/ede519f9548509695f71af0b4da4133ecee6a7096f8cef693396c93f0c93b6a8" "q-85993/selection/0444fcd60c99f1c7d68f1ec72c0edfc4dba58137fbf65ca1a3b6f87a282e3baa" "q-85993/selection/93b0e7d6c722cdad57903f6e553c2a90130576e683447672e639acdf5731f41b" "q-85993/selection/ddcdb16f55dd87ecf4915bdee8381889bdbe47ba511833137a4f0eb2ddc785a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-85993" :question/id #uuid "8ce7302d-756f-51f6-96fc-8dd63d5b081e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85993" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand $f(x)={x}^{21}-16{x}^{19}+6x-3$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda ) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{19}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-16\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{21}\\,-\\,16{x}^{19}\\,+\\,6x\\,\\,-\\,\\,3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{21}$ | $-$ | $16{x}^{19}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $6x$ | $-$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(6x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{19}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(6A-3I) \\\\  & =6A-3I \\\\  & =6[\\begin{aligned}4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -24\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & 72 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-85993/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-85993"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140602/selection/7f4fad57b4b4898e257fd5bb49b94df0d6ea93bb5af8530dbc7389cf89d850b4" :answer/id #uuid "cc8f8ff8-1fc7-5703-93e5-8fbed535e604" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & -42 \\\\ 14 & -31\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140602/selection/fa32240d7c892d366494817909a43aefb1a93890d0bfeb302415cba60d91acc8" :answer/id #uuid "1b39e8f6-0474-5c6f-b312-0ef2bee294ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}31 & 14 \\\\ 42 & -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140602/selection/2ecfbcf8670113a289ed97be6a6cb21643aad5c937182fe6e24af082cf2eceaa" :answer/id #uuid "15a26715-cfc2-5601-af13-80bf22ec0943" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140602/selection/fa5683fbc3fdaaab1b33d5dd922fb154dd87b741567fafb1a1e8c5612125a9bd" :answer/id #uuid "f307dc9e-141f-5c15-8531-f742680d06a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 14 \\\\ -42 & 30\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140602/selection/946883a75f690115acc6af99363162ebcd8a179bbc37bbc3b4cb75464cb4426d" :answer/id #uuid "e62e7d44-5f44-56a2-9946-9f00457595bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & 12 \\\\ -24 & -31\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140602/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d1fd8c4-4982-5b73-bc7e-d1a4c49688bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140602/selection/2ecfbcf8670113a289ed97be6a6cb21643aad5c937182fe6e24af082cf2eceaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-140602/selection/7f4fad57b4b4898e257fd5bb49b94df0d6ea93bb5af8530dbc7389cf89d850b4" "q-140602/selection/fa32240d7c892d366494817909a43aefb1a93890d0bfeb302415cba60d91acc8" "q-140602/selection/2ecfbcf8670113a289ed97be6a6cb21643aad5c937182fe6e24af082cf2eceaa" "q-140602/selection/fa5683fbc3fdaaab1b33d5dd922fb154dd87b741567fafb1a1e8c5612125a9bd" "q-140602/selection/946883a75f690115acc6af99363162ebcd8a179bbc37bbc3b4cb75464cb4426d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140602" :question/id #uuid "90d489f4-f1b4-5826-a3b4-cd8aff951e6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140602" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{16}-4{x}^{14}+7x-3\\,$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+12 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{14}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-4\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{16}\\,-\\,4{x}^{14}\\,+\\,7x\\,-\\,3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{16}$ | $-$ | $4{x}^{14}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $7x$ | $-$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{14}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =7A-3I \\\\  & =7[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -28\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-140602/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140602"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5894"] :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/cb48b48ef82e5d6cfe903488e80a8cbd48e286c864ba387450e01430e718d77c.png) find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda),$ i.e., the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5511"] :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\textrm{tr}(A)=-4$ and $\\textrm{det}(A)=-18.$ If $A^2 = xA + yI$ for constants $x$ and $y,$ then find the value of $x+y.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2-\\textrm{tr}(A)\\lambda+\\textrm{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-4A+18I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y = -4+18 = 14.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5510"] :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/b2240a11f1760050914a9c02554e0b7e86cd84d420fb437a43d95b474103b26c.png) and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+54 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}+A-2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}+A-2I) & =O \\\\ A+I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $q=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}=1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5512"] :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14058184/assets/308850190e8483ae48e8179585c01f1805e6caf92e4d4254c996bd0ffa800427.png) and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1,$ find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+15 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\:\\:x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x^2-1\\:$ | $\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\, - \\, x^{10}  +  2x  -  1 {\\phantom{|}} }$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $x^{12}$ | $-$ | $x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $2x$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{10}({x}^{2}-1)+(2x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x) = x^{10}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-I) \\\\  & =2A-I \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
